# Regression test: `echo`'s printing of a table_input_stream value used to happen on a
# raw, untracked std::thread::Builder thread (src/lang/pretty.rs's PrettyPrinter::print_value)
# that was spawned and never joined. Because that thread wasn't registered with
# ThreadStore, `echo`'s own CommandContext::eval() call returned as soon as the thread was
# spawned rather than once it actually finished printing, so `Job::eval` (and therefore
# execute.rs's `source()` statement loop) considered the statement complete while the
# stream was still being printed in the background. The next top-level statement then
# started running -- and printing its own output -- concurrently with the still-running
# background thread from the previous statement, racing on the single global Printer
# channel and producing interleaved/out-of-order stdout.
#
# `zip $(seq 3000) $(seq 3000) | echo` produces enough rows (and enough print-batches,
# since the pretty printer flushes every ~29 rows) to give the race a wide, reliable
# window; the very next statement, `echo "after"`, reliably lands in the middle of the
# stream's output instead of after it when the bug is present.

zip $(seq 3000) $(seq 3000) | echo
echo "after"
